talk(562,'1982-09-06','A. B. Zamolodchikov','P001029-P001034','Classical string constraints and cyclic invariant charges',r'The entry proper begins on P001030 and ends with the first lines of P001033. It moves from the Nambu--Goto action and Dirac constraints through two Witt algebras to oscillator quantization and the Virasoro anomaly. It then asks for the quantum algebra of cyclic iterated integrals. The classical construction, the asserted invariance of naively normally ordered operators, abstract deformation quantization, and completeness are distinct items with different later fates.')
add(562,'Lorentzian area action and its sign','P001030','definition',r'The target metric is \(\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,\ldots,-1)\), while the integrand is \(\sqrt{\dot X^2(X^{\prime})^2-(\dot X\cdot X^{\prime})^2}\).',r'For a timelike immersed worldsheet with induced metric \(h_{ab}=\partial_aX\cdot\partial_bX\), a real action is \(S=-T\int\sqrt{-\det h}\,d\tau\,d\sigma=-T\int\sqrt{(\dot X\cdot X^{\prime})^2-\dot X^2(X^{\prime})^2}\,d\tau\,d\sigma\), \(T>0\). Degenerate worldsheets need separate treatment.',r'This is established string mechanics; \citet[Section1.2]{tong2009string} supplies an explicitly normalized reference. The square-root sign follows directly from Lorentzian signature.',('tong2009string',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The tension, timelike immersion and real square-root convention are supplied.')
add(562,'Singular Legendre transform and first-class constraints','P001030-P001031','construction',r'The canonical Hamiltonian vanishes. Constraints are first \(p^2-(X^{\prime})^2=0,\ p\cdot X^{\prime}=0\), then \((p\pm X^{\prime})^2=0\); a Hamiltonian is formed from arbitrary multipliers.',r'For the Lorentzian area action, \(p^2+T^2(X^{\prime})^2=0\) and \(p\cdot X^{\prime}=0\). The canonical Hamiltonian vanishes by degree-one velocity homogeneity. The total Hamiltonian is a linear combination of these first-class constraints; lapse and shift encode worldsheet reparametrization.',r'Dirac developed the framework in August1949 lectures and \citet[Sections2--4]{dirac1950generalized}. The string specialization is checked by differentiating the action.',('dirac1950generalized',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='Tension T is restored and the conflicting sign is repaired.')
add(562,'Two classical Witt algebras and Fourier conventions','P001031','theorem',r'Modes of \((p+X^{\prime})^2\) and \((p-X^{\prime})^2\) close as commuting Witt algebras. \(X\) and \(p\) use opposite Fourier signs, but their mode bracket uses \(\delta_{n+m,0}\).',r'The smeared chiral constraints have the brackets of vector fields on \(S^1\), with opposite orientation conventions for the chiralities and zero mixed bracket. If \(X=\sum x_ne^{in\sigma}\), \(p=\sum p_me^{-im\sigma}\), normalization aside \(\{x_n,p_m\}=\delta_{nm}\); same-sign expansions give \(\delta_{n+m,0}\).',r'This is the classical constraint calculation underlying \citet[Sections1.4 and2.1]{tong2009string}. Fourier orthogonality gives the delta correction. First-class closure also proves preservation of the constraints by multiplier evolution.',('tong2009string',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The algebraic claim and the inconsistent Fourier convention are separated; factors of \(2\pi\) depend on normalization.')
add(562,'Oscillators, Fock space and the Virasoro central term','P001031-P001032','construction',r'Oscillators combine \(p_n,x_n\), a vacuum is annihilated for \(n\ge0\), and quadratic modes acquire \(D(n^3-n)/12\). Individual Fourier modes are called Hermitian.',r'Normalize \([\alpha_m^\mu,\alpha_n^\nu]=m\eta^{\mu\nu}\delta_{m+n,0}\), \((\alpha_n^\mu)^\dagger=\alpha_{-n}^\mu\), \(\alpha_n|0;p\rangle=0\) for \(n>0\), and \(L_n=\tfrac12\sum_m:\alpha_{n-m}\cdot\alpha_m:\). On finite excitations, \([L_m,L_n]=(m-n)L_{m+n}+\tfrac D{12}(m^3-m)\delta_{m+n,0}\). The zero mode is momentum, not an oscillator annihilator.',r'The oscillator construction predates1982; \citet[Section2.1]{tong2009string} makes conventions explicit. Reality, mode factors and the zero-mode qualification are distinct defects.',('tong2009string',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='A common algebraic domain, Minkowski contraction, one-half factor and zero-mode sector are explicit.')
add(562,'Incompatibility of all quantum Virasoro constraints','P001032','claim',r'The notes say that all \(L_n\Psi=0\) are incompatible with the central term.',r'If \(c\ne0\) and every \(L_n v=0\) on a common invariant domain, then \(0=[L_2,L_{-2}]v=(4L_0+c/2)v=(c/2)v\), hence \(v=0\). This is a statement before canceling matter and ghost central charges.',r'The claim follows immediately from the known Virasoro relation; \citet[Section2.1.2]{tong2009string} discusses the physical constraint prescription.',('tong2009string',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The elementary proof and the distinction from the total anomaly-free BRST complex are editorial.')
add(562,'Half-constraints and physical matrix elements','P001032','method',r'Only \(L_n\Psi=0\) for \(n\ge0\) are imposed; every constraint is then said to have vanishing physical matrix elements.',r'With \(L_n^\dagger=L_{-n}\), \(L_n v=0\) for \(n>0\) and \((L_0-a)v=0\) imply \(\langle u,L_nv\rangle=0\) for \(n\ne0\) and \(\langle u,(L_0-a)v\rangle=0\) between physical vectors. These conditions alone prove neither positivity nor consistency in arbitrary dimension.',r'This is the old covariant prescription with the intercept suppressed; see \citet[Section2.1.2]{tong2009string}. Conjugation proves the negative-mode assertion.',('tong2009string',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='The intercept and limits of the matrix-element conclusion are restored.')
add(562,'Cyclic iterated integrals as classical string observables','P001032-P001033','construction',r'Ordered products of \(y^\mu=p^\mu+iX^{\prime\mu}\) are integrated over cyclically ordered points. Their \(Q\)'+'s are said to commute with constraints and momentum; the classical bracket has length \(n+m-2\).',r'For a closed classical string put \(u_\pm=p\pm TX^{\prime}\). Sum ordered iterated integrals over cyclic permutations of tensor indices. These charges are independent of the starting point, invariant under orientation-preserving reparametrizations and conserved on shell. Their span, shuffle multiplication and induced Poisson bracket form a Poisson algebra; the bracket contracts one index from each word.',r'This matches the Pohlmeyer--Rehren construction, explicitly given in \citet[Equations1.1--1.2 and Section2]{bahnsmeinecke2011string}. Its first cited publication is Pohlmeyer1982; accessible evidence did not date circulation before this September talk. Later algebraic development is by others, hence B.',('bahnsmeinecke2011string',),cl='B',match='exact',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The real Lorentzian currents replace the unexplained printed \(i\). The length rule is schematic: coefficients, contractions and shuffle relations remain necessary.',status='the classical cyclic-charge Poisson algebra is established.')
add(562,'Normal ordering does not preserve all charge relations','P001032','claim',r'After adding normal-ordering colons, the notes assert \([Q,L_n]=0\).',r'Expand classical charges in oscillators and normally order formally. Their constraint commutators and defining relations still require verification. Naive canonical normal ordering does not in general yield the invariant algebra with only observable quantum corrections.',r'\citet[Sections4--5]{bahns2004string} finds anomalous terms which cannot be removed by changing target dimension. This negative result concerns that prescription, not every deformation quantization.',('bahns2004string',),cl='B',literal='false as written',match='exact',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The universal quantum assertion is separated from classical invariance and alternative quantizations.',status='the unqualified canonical normal-ordering assertion fails.')
add(562,'The quantum algebra of cyclic charges','P001032-P001033','question',r'Zamolodchikov asks for the mutual commutation relations of the \(Q\)'+'s, after stating the classical length rule.',r'For the cyclic-charge Poisson algebra \(\mathfrak h\), construct an associative formal deformation over \(\mathbb C[[\hbar]]\) with zeroth product the shuffle product and first commutator \(i\hbar\{\, ,\,\}\), and describe its quantum relations. The notes impose no Hilbert-space representation requirement.',r'\citet[Theorem1.1]{enriquezhalbout2010quantization}, circulated2008 and published2010, constructs quasi-Lie-bialgebra quantization. \citet[Proposition15 and p.~20]{bahnsmeinecke2011string} explicitly deduces existence for \(\mathfrak h\) without quadratic generation. This substantial algebraic answer does not validate the notebook operators.',('enriquezhalbout2010quantization','bahnsmeinecke2011string'),cl='B',match='partial',verification='read the result',problem='GP-1982-01',reconstruction='Formal deformation and the semiclassical condition specify the algebraic question. Preferred explicit presentations and positive representations are stronger requirements.',status='abstract formal quantization exists; the question is not catalogued as an open existence problem.')
add(562,'Completeness of invariant charges','P001033','claim',r'The continuation calls \(\{Q\}\) a complete set of invariants commuting with \(P^\mu\).',r'Completeness requires a specified class of worldsheets and an equivalence by translations and reparametrization. It does not assert free algebraic independence or generation of every momentum-commuting function without regularity restrictions.',r'\citet[pp.~177--186]{pohlmeyerrehren1988completeness} treats the exact geometric question (receivedApril1987, publishedJune1988). Its opened abstract establishes the match; \citet[Section1]{bahns2004string} explains auxiliary boosts and translations along total momentum. The original full hypotheses were inaccessible.',('pohlmeyerrehren1988completeness','bahns2004string'),cl='B',match='exact',verification='indirect',confidence='medium',reconstruction='Completeness is interpreted geometrically, without promoting the abbreviated assertion to an unrestricted theorem.',status='a geometric completeness theorem exists for its stated class; the notebook omits necessary scope and auxiliary data.')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-01',title='Quantization of cyclic invariant charges of the closed string',statement=r'Construct and describe an associative formal quantum deformation of the Poisson algebra of cyclic iterated integrals of closed-string chiral currents, with prescribed classical bracket.',occurrences=['1982-562.9'],status=r'As of November2026, abstract existence follows from quasi-Lie-bialgebra quantization, with the string application stated by Bahns--Meinecke. Naive canonical normal ordering fails. A preferred explicit presentation or positive representation is a stronger target than the recorded existence question.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['enriquezhalbout2010quantization','bahnsmeinecke2011string','bahns2004string']))
corr('P001030','Lorentzian area integrand',r'\(\sqrt{\dot X^2(X^{\prime})^2-(\dot X\cdot X^{\prime})^2}\).',r'Use \(\sqrt{(\dot X\cdot X^{\prime})^2-\dot X^2(X^{\prime})^2}\) for a timelike worldsheet.','Russian and scan1030 checked. The displayed induced determinant is negative for Lorentzian signature.')
corr('P001030','Legendre-transform constraint',r'\(p^2-(X^{\prime})^2=0\).',r'\(p^2+T^2(X^{\prime})^2=0\), T=1 in the notes.','Scans1030–31 checked; expanding both (p+X prime)^2=(p-X prime)^2=0 forces the plus sign.')
corr('P001031','Fourier canonical delta',r'Opposite Fourier signs with \(\delta_{n+m,0}\).',r'Use \(\delta_{nm}\), or same-sign expansions.','Russian and scan1031 checked; direct Fourier orthogonality.')
corr('P001031','Reality and oscillator indices',r'Individual modes Hermitian; equation18 repeats n on the left, \(\delta_{nm}\) on the right.',r'Use \(x_n^\dagger=x_{-n}\), \(p_n^\dagger=p_{-n}\), distinct n,m indices, target metric and consistent mode factors.','Scan1031 inspected; a real periodic field has conjugate opposite modes.')
corr('P001032','Vacuum condition',r'\(a_n\Psi_0=0\) for \(n\ge0\).',r'Positive oscillators annihilate the vacuum; specify zero-mode momentum separately.','Scan1032 checked against the center-of-mass momentum on the same page.')
